course: dictionary
lesson: 9918
title: A word about growing up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are about your body, and they can feel new or awkward. Context helps. Look at the words around them. «A tampon is a small soft roll of cotton that soaks up blood during a period.» Soaks up tells you it takes in liquid, and a period is the monthly time when blood leaves the body.
explain: Long, official words can sound cold, so they are harder to understand. A shop sign says «feminine hygiene products». Swap in your meaning: it simply means pads and tampons. The swap check works here, and the sentence makes sense.
explain: Tampons, pads and period pants are all ways to manage periods. If you have questions, ask a trusted adult, your parent or a school nurse. Learning the words helps you feel more confident about growing up.
fact: A tampon is a small soft roll of cotton or similar material.
fact: It goes inside the vagina and soaks up blood during a period.
fact: Tampons are sold in packs at shops, and some school bathrooms have them.
fact: Change a tampon every few hours, as health lessons teach, to stay safe from a rare but serious illness called toxic shock syndrome.
fact: «Feminine hygiene products» means pads and tampons, which are ways to manage periods.
exercise: «A tampon is a small soft roll of cotton put inside the body to soak up blood.» What does soak up mean? | Hide from view | Throw away | Make it bigger | *Take in liquid
exercise: The shop sign says «feminine hygiene products». What does it simply mean? | Shoes and socks | *Pads and tampons | Cups for drinks | Soap and shampoo
exercise: Which word means the monthly time when blood leaves the body? | Holiday | Season | *Period | Birthday
test: Why does the sign say «feminine hygiene products» instead of pads and tampons? | Only doctors are allowed to use it | Pads and tampons are not sold in shops | It is the shortest name for the products | *The long official name sounds less personal
test: Which safety tip comes from the health lesson? | *Change a tampon every few hours | Look at the packet and change it once a day only | Never tell an adult | Keep a tampon in for a whole week
exam: What is the best way to check a word's meaning in a sentence? | Count the letters in the word | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Ignore the sentence and guess | Ask a friend to write it for you
